task: growing-adopted-part0
agent: opus5-bench
started: 2026-09-08
branch: feature/growing-adopted-part0
worktree: ../okay-wt-part0

why: `growing` reads 3.6x the `adaptive` it grows into, and my own
  backlog entry named the adopted part 0 as the suspect BY
  ELIMINATION. Before touching that, I checked what the two lanes
  actually build, and they are not the same object:

    growing_chunk   Growing(Ring(1024), 16, () => Ring(1024/16))
                    part 0 = 1024, fifteen more at 64  ->  1 984 slots
    adaptive_chunk  AdaptiveFifo(16, () => Ring(1024))
                    sixteen parts at 1024              -> 16 384 slots

  `adaptive` has 8.3x the buffer. With 8 000 elements and one
  consumer, capacity decides how often a producer parks and how large
  the consumer's batches are, so a large part of the "3.6x" may be
  capacity and not mechanism. That is a MISMATCHED PAIR — the seventh
  instance of this class on this page and the second I have filed
  myself.

what: price it before designing anything. Add one diagnostic lane,
  `adaptiveSmall_chunk` = `adaptive.parts(16).each(Cap / 16)`, which
  gives `adaptive` parts the size `growing`'s grown parts actually
  are. Measure it beside `growing_chunk` and `adaptive_chunk` on a
  quiet box, alternating.

predict: `adaptiveSmall` lands far closer to `growing` than
  `adaptive` does, and most of the 3.6x is the capacity difference.
  DISQUALIFYING: if `adaptiveSmall` still reads near `adaptive`'s
  117 at sixteen producers, capacity is NOT the story, the adopted
  part 0 goes back on the table, and this entry keeps its original
  shape. Either way the backlog entry gets corrected, because the
  pairing it was filed on is wrong regardless of the answer.
